    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d64">

                <pb facs="0069" n="55" sameAs="frus:frus1947v06/pg55"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">851G.00/1–417: Circular telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of state to certain Diplomatic Officers
                        </hi><anchor n="16" xml:id="d64-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1947-01-07T15:00:00-05:00">January 7, 1947—3
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Nav Attaché Bangkok<anchor n="17" xml:id="d64-f2"/> reports French now trying
                    clear lines Saigon to Cambodian border using all French Indochina reserves.
                    These troops and 16,000 now en route from France then be thrown into all out
                    reconquest Tonkin where puppet Govt may be set up. This is plan of d’Argenlieu
                    and LeClerc,<anchor n="18" xml:id="d64-f3"/> with Moutet wavering as leftists
                    Saigou reportedly favor reopening negotiations with Ho. Final decision be made
                    when Moutet returns Paris Jan 16.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Byrnes</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d64-f1" n="16" type="source" xml:id="d64-f1-content">
                <p>At Paris.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d64-f2" n="17" xml:id="d64-f2-content">
                <p>Comdr. Alfred W. Gardes, Jr.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d64-f3" n="18" xml:id="d64-f3-content">
                <p>Maj. Gen. Jacques-Philippe Leclerc, Inspector General of French Armies in North
                    Africa, formerly Commander in Chief of French Forces in the Far East.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
